==============================================================================
WARNINGS ON THE INVENTORY ITSELF
==============================================================================

  1. THE LOCK FILE IS NOT USED BY THE BUILD
     The Dockerfile copies only package.json:
       COPY package.json /
       RUN cd / && npm install
     package-lock.json sits beside the Dockerfile but is never copied into the
     build context of the image, so `npm install` resolves the dependency tree
     afresh against the semver ranges in package.json every time the image is
     built. The inventory in LICENSE and NOTICE was derived from the committed
     package-lock.json, which is the only machine-readable record of the tree
     in this repository. IT IS NOT GUARANTEED TO MATCH WHAT THE IMAGE SHIPS.
     Copy package-lock.json into the image and use `npm ci` so that the
     licensed set and the installed set are the same.

  2. EVERY DEPENDENCY IS A CARET RANGE
     package.json pins nothing: aws-s3 ^2.0.5, minio ^8.0.7, mongodb ^7.6.0,
     ollama ^0.6.3, openai ^7.10.0, openwhisk ^3.21.9, pg ^8.23.0, redis
     ^6.2.1, uuid ^14.0.2. A rebuild can therefore pull new minor versions and
     entirely new transitive packages whose licenses nobody has reviewed. The
     82 packages recorded here are the tree as locked, not a ceiling.

     runtime/nodejs/v20 ships the identical package.json with a lock file that
     resolves to a different and older tree: 119 packages, including mongodb
     6.21.0, openai 4.104.0, redis 4.7.1 and uuid 9.0.1, none of which satisfy
     the ranges declared for them. The two images are therefore licensed over
     two different dependency sets from the same manifest.

  3. TWO PACKAGES SHIP NO LICENSE TEXT
     pg-types 2.2.0 and pgpass 1.0.5 declare "MIT" in their package.json but
     publish no LICENSE file, and their repositories do not carry one either. The MIT license requires its copyright notice to be
     reproduced with redistribution; for these three there is no copyright
     notice to reproduce. They are disclosed in LICENSE and NOTICE with the
     declared license and that fact. Raise it with the upstream projects, or
     replace them, before relying on this image in a release.

  4. THE npm INVENTORY WAS BUILT FROM PUBLISHED TARBALLS, NOT FROM THE IMAGE
     The license texts concatenated into LICENSE were taken from the package
     tarballs on the npm registry at exactly the versions in
     package-lock.json, except for eleven packages that publish no license
     file in the tarball, whose texts come from their upstream repositories.
     The authoritative copy for the shipped image remains
     /node_modules/<package>/LICENSE inside the image.

  5. THE RUNTIME PACKAGE DECLARES THE WRONG LICENSE
     package.json declares "license": "ISC" for the OpenServerless nodejs
     runtime package itself. This repository is Apache-2.0. The declaration is
     not shipped to users as a license grant, but it misstates the license of
     ASF code and should be corrected to "Apache-2.0".

  6. DELEGATE AND TRANSITIVE OPERATING-SYSTEM LIBRARIES ARE NOT ENUMERATED
     `apt-get install imagemagick graphicsmagick unzip` pulls a large set of
     further Debian packages -- the image and compression delegate libraries
     that both Magick implementations link against -- which are installed into
     the image and are not listed in LICENSE or NOTICE. Their copyright files
     are in /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright inside the image. They are part
     of the convenience binary, not of the source release; a complete
     per-package inventory can only be produced from a built image.

  7. LICENSE TEXTS FOR THE BASE IMAGE WERE TAKEN FROM UPSTREAM
     The Node.js LICENSE reproduced here is the one published at tag v22.23.2,
     which matches the pinned base image. The npm, Yarn, ImageMagick and
     GraphicsMagick texts come from those projects' own repositories and may
     be a different revision from the one Debian ships. The authoritative copy
     for the shipped binaries remains /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright and
     the base image's own license files.

  8. THE BUNDLED PROXY CANNOT BE VERIFIED FROM THIS DIRECTORY
     /bin/proxy is copied from the image named by the COMMON build argument,
     which defaults to `missing:missing`. The Go module inventory in LICENSE
     and NOTICE was derived from the go.mod and go.sum of this repository. If
     the image passed as COMMON is built from a different source tree, the
     modules actually linked into /bin/proxy may differ.

  9. DEPENDENCIES OF USER ACTIONS ARE NOT COVERED
     Beyond the packages listed above, user actions bring their own npm
     dependencies. Those libraries are NOT part of this image and NOT part of
     any Apache release artifact, and no claim is made here about their
     licenses.


==============================================================================
ADDITIONAL NOTES
==============================================================================

The base image node:22.23.2-bookworm is pinned to a patch release while the
apt and npm steps are not pinned at all, so the image mixes a frozen runtime
with a floating dependency set.

This image is the default Node.js runtime: its merge.json sets "default": true
and declares the stem-cell pool configuration.
